            <ArticleTitle>Restenosis after percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>149-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Restenosis after percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty was demonstrated in 61 (29%) of 210 successfully treated patients. Mostly it occurred within 4 months after treatment and in arteries less than 3 mm in diameter. Careful clinical follow-up is therefore particularly important early after angioplasty of smaller arteries. Redilation can be performed without increased risk of restenosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
